{"text":"MODEL_FEEDBACK_JSON:
{
  "positive_alignment_reasons": [
    "Push buttons and potentiometers feel useful because they add manual controls to the Arduino circuits I have built with breadboards, PCB parts, and a relay.",
    "The clock module feels like a natural next experiment because it could add timed operation to my relay projects. Ferrite cores also connect to components I have bought before, provided their specifications fit my circuit."
  ],
  "negative_misalignment_reasons": [
    "Laser modules and a high-voltage probe serve specific tasks I have not shown a need for; buying electronics components does not mean I need every kind of electronic device.",
    "The 18650 wraps do not match my previous 9V battery holder, and potassium nitrate has no clear connection to the projects reflected in my history."
  ],
  "overgeneralized_signals": [
    "The suggestions seem to treat a broad connection to electronics as enough, even when the part has no clear role in my current projects.",
    "Past component purchases seem to be taken as a reason to offer large assortments, although I may already have enough LEDs and switches from my PCB kit."
  ],
  "missing_or_underweighted_signals": [
    "I am more likely to choose a part when I can identify a concrete use for it, such as manual adjustment or timed relay control, than simply because it belongs in an electronics toolkit.",
    "Compatibility and quantity matter: I would check ferrite specifications, distinguish 9V hardware from 18650 accessories, and wait for a specific need before buying hundreds of extra components."
  ],
  "summary_diagnosis": "The suggestions recognize my interest in hands-on electronics but sometimes miss the project purpose, compatibility, and quantity that determine whether I would choose a part now."
}"}
